// Key takeaways (k) and reasons a book was recommended (w), from the Notion Reading Tracker.
window.READING_NOTES = {
"A Grown-Up Guide to Dinosaurs": {k: "For adults who never outgrew their dinosaur phase, Garrod updates everything you thought you knew with the latest paleontological discoveries. From feathered raptors to revised extinction theories, the science has changed dramatically. A fun, accessible science listen that brings childhood fascination up to date."},
"A History of the World in 6 Glasses": {k: "Standage traces human civilization through six beverages: beer, wine, spirits, coffee, tea, and Coca-Cola, each defining an era. From Mesopotamian beer halls to British tea colonies to American soft-drink globalization, drinks shaped culture, commerce, and politics. A clever, accessible history that shows how what we drink reveals who we are."},
"A Thousand Splendid Suns": {k: "Two Afghan women from different generations — Mariam, an illegitimate child married off at 15, and Laila, a bright girl orphaned by war — are forced to share a brutal husband. Against the backdrop of 30 years of war, their bond becomes their only source of strength. A heartbreaking novel about female endurance, sacrifice, and love surviving in the harshest conditions.", w: "You loved The Kite Runner - arguably even more powerful. Devastating and unforgettable."},
"A Woman Is No Man": {k: "Three generations of Palestinian-American women struggle against the expectations of a patriarchal culture in Brooklyn. Isra, a young bride from Palestine, discovers that the American dream doesn't apply to women in her community. A devastating novel about silence, abuse, and the courage it takes for women to claim their own stories."},
"ADHD Is Awesome": {k: "A husband-and-wife team, one with ADHD and one without, walk through the condition's practical realities — time blindness, rejection sensitivity, executive dysfunction — alongside systems that actually help. The tone is upbeat and self-deprecating rather than clinical, with an introduction by Edward Hallowell for the medical grounding. Aimed at people who have bounced off drier ADHD books."},
"All Fours": {k: "A married woman in her mid-40s sets out on a solo road trip but instead checks into a motel 30 minutes from home and begins a secret reinvention of her life. She navigates perimenopause, desire, creative ambition, and the suffocation of domesticity with raw honesty. A bold, polarizing novel about female midlife that refuses to be polite about aging, sex, and wanting more."},
"American Psycho": {k: "Patrick Bateman is a wealthy, image-obsessed Wall Street investment banker in 1980s Manhattan who may also be a serial killer — or may be fantasizing the whole thing. The novel blurs the line between satire and horror as consumerism and violence become indistinguishable. A deliberately repulsive, deeply polarizing critique of American materialism that dares you to look away."},
"Antigone": {k: "Antigone defies King Creon's decree to leave her brother's body unburied, choosing divine law and family duty over the state. The clash between individual conscience and political authority ends in tragedy for everyone. A 2,500-year-old play that remains the definitive drama about civil disobedience and the cost of moral courage."},
"Atomic Habits": {k: "Tiny changes compound into remarkable results — getting 1% better daily matters more than radical overhauls. Clear introduces the four laws of behavior change: make it obvious, attractive, easy, and satisfying. A practical system for building good habits and breaking bad ones by focusing on identity rather than outcomes."},
"Audible Sleep Weekly": {k: "Weekly sleep content: stories, sound baths, meditations."},
"Babel": {k: "In an alternate 1830s Oxford, translation and silver bars power the British Empire through magic, and a Chinese orphan named Robin is recruited to serve this system. As he excels academically, he discovers the empire's magic depends on exploiting colonized languages and peoples. A sweeping fantasy about colonialism, language, and whether revolution can exist without violence."},
"Bad Blood": {k: "The inside story of how Elizabeth Holmes built Theranos into a $9 billion startup on the promise of revolutionary blood-testing technology that never worked. Employees who raised alarms were silenced through intimidation and legal threats. A gripping tale of Silicon Valley hubris, fraud, and how charisma and ambition can override truth for terrifyingly long."},
"Being Mortal": {k: "Gawande confronts medicine's failure to deal honestly with aging and death, arguing that doctors prioritize treatment over quality of life. He explores how nursing homes, hospice, and end-of-life conversations could be radically better. A profound book that asks what makes life worth living when the body is failing — essential reading for anyone in healthcare.", w: "You loved Complications - this is Gawande's best and most important work. It will fundamentally change how you think about end-of-life care. Essential for anyone in healthcare."},
"Bernoulli's Fallacy": {k: "Clayton argues that the dominant approach to statistics — frequentism — is built on a logical error dating back to Jacob Bernoulli, and that this flaw underlies the reproducibility crisis in science. He makes the case for Bayesian reasoning as a more coherent foundation for scientific inference. A challenging but rewarding book for anyone who wants to understand why so many published studies turn out to be wrong."},
"Better": {k: "Gawande explores what it takes to perform well in medicine — examining hand-washing compliance, battlefield surgeons, malpractice, and the bell curve of doctor performance. He argues that the difference between average and excellent comes down to diligence, doing right, and ingenuity. A companion to Complications that asks not just how medicine fails, but how it gets better."},
"Between the World and Me": {k: "Written as a letter to his teenage son, Coates reflects on what it means to live in a Black body in America — the constant vulnerability, the history of plunder, and the myth of the American Dream. Drawing from personal experience and American history, he argues that racism is a physical, bodily threat, not just an abstract idea. A searing, essential work that won the National Book Award and changed the national conversation about race.", w: "Only 3.5 hours. Pairs with Born a Crime and Just Mercy which you already loved. Essential, brief, and beautifully written."},
"Between Two Kingdoms": {k: "At 22, Jaouad was diagnosed with leukemia and spent nearly four years in treatment, documenting her journey in a New York Times column. After remission, she embarks on a 15,000-mile road trip to visit strangers who wrote to her during illness. A memoir about how surviving isn't the same as living, and the hard work of rebuilding an identity after crisis."},
"Black Heart": {k: "When a banker is found dead in a luxury hotel bathtub, Detective Dan Riley investigates what initially looks like a suicide but quickly becomes more sinister. The victim's double life reveals a web of secrets, blackmail, and dangerous liaisons. A pacy British crime thriller with twists that keep you guessing."},
"Blood Orange Night": {k: "Bond's doctor prescribed benzodiazepines for insomnia during her pregnancy, and she quickly became physically dependent without realizing it. As her body deteriorated and her life unraveled, she discovered how widespread and under-acknowledged benzo dependence is. A harrowing memoir about the medical system's failure to protect patients from the drugs it prescribes."},
"Body of Proof": {k: "A true crime documentary about a woman who vanishes on her way to work and the man convicted of her murder — despite key evidence being missing. The case raises troubling questions about whether the right person is behind bars. A gripping investigation into justice, reasonable doubt, and what happens when the prosecution's story has holes."},
"Born a Crime": {k: "Noah's existence as a mixed-race child was literally a crime under apartheid, and his memoir traces growing up in South Africa with a fearless, defiant mother. He navigates poverty, racial absurdity, and identity with humor that makes the darkest moments bearable. One of the best audiobook performances ever — funny, moving, and illuminating about a world most listeners never knew."},
"Braiding Sweetgrass": {k: "A botanist and member of the Citizen Potawatomi Nation weaves together indigenous wisdom and scientific knowledge about plants. Kimmerer argues that plants are not just resources but gifts, and that reciprocity with nature is essential. A lyrical call to shift from an extractive relationship with the earth to one of gratitude and mutual care."},
"Buried Deep": {k: "When police reopen an investigation into a mysterious death, Maggie Cabot refuses to sit by as suspicion falls on her husband. She begins her own investigation into his past. A short, twisty domestic thriller for fans of Big Little Lies."},
"Caffeine": {k: "Pollan explores how caffeine shaped the modern world — fueling the Enlightenment, the Industrial Revolution, and our current productivity culture. He quits coffee for three months and documents the withdrawal and the revelation of how dependent we all are. A fascinating short listen about humanity's most widely used drug and why we can't quit it."},
"Can't Take It Back": {k: "Four women's lives intersect over the course of a kindergarten school year, as past mistakes and present tensions collide in their tight-knit community. When secrets start surfacing, each must decide what's worth fighting for and what to let go. A fast-paced, relatable story about motherhood, friendship, and the impossibility of perfection."},
"Carmilla": {k: "A young woman in an isolated castle is visited by a mysterious, alluring guest named Carmilla — who turns out to be something far more dangerous than a friend. Written in 1872, this gothic novella predated Dracula and introduced the vampire as a figure of seduction. A classic of horror literature, beautifully produced with a full cast including David Tennant."},
"Christmas Eve, 1914": {k: "Based on the real Christmas Truce of World War I, British officers on the front lines experience an extraordinary night of peace with the German enemy just 80 yards away. Full cast audio drama that captures the absurdity and humanity of war. A moving, beautifully produced piece about the moments when our shared humanity overrides the machinery of conflict."},
"Clinical Decision Support": {k: "A comprehensive academic text on how clinical decision support systems can improve healthcare quality, safety, and efficiency. Covers the technical, organizational, and policy challenges of implementing CDS in real clinical settings. A specialized reference for healthcare informatics professionals — dense but authoritative."},
"Compassionomics": {k: "Two physician-scientists compile extensive evidence that compassion in healthcare isn't just nice — it measurably improves patient outcomes, reduces costs, and prevents burnout. They argue that compassion is a skill that can be taught, not just an innate trait. A data-driven case that caring is the missing vital sign in modern medicine."},
"Complications": {k: "A surgeon reveals the messy, uncertain reality behind medicine — how doctors learn on the job, how mistakes happen, and how much remains unknown. Gawande explores cases where the system fails and the human factors that complicate even routine procedures. A humbling, honest look at medicine as an imperfect science practiced by imperfect humans."},
"Conditional Citizens": {k: "Lalami, a Moroccan-born American citizen, examines how race, religion, and gender determine whose citizenship is fully honored and whose is conditional. She blends memoir with history and policy analysis to expose the gap between American ideals and lived reality. A piercing essay on belonging and who gets to claim the full promise of We the People."},
"Cues": {k: "Van Edwards breaks charisma into two measurable signals — warmth and competence — and catalogs the vocal, verbal, and body-language cues that convey each. The material draws on behavioral research and is aimed squarely at professional settings: meetings, pitches, interviews. Practical to the point of being a checklist, for better or worse."},
"Cut and Run": {k: "Meg Ryan leads a full cast in this dark comedy about two best friends who steal kidneys for a living — and have their reasons. When their operation goes sideways, loyalties are tested. A wild, funny audio original with a stacked cast."},
"Dark Matter": {k: "A physicist is kidnapped and wakes up in an alternate version of his life where he made different choices — and now he must fight across infinite realities to get back to his family. The central question: are you defined by the choices you made, or the ones you didn't? A mind-bending thriller that's impossible to put down, exploring identity and the multiverse."},
"Deep Medicine": {k: "Topol argues that AI won't replace doctors but will free them from data drudgery to focus on what matters: the human connection with patients. He surveys AI applications across diagnosis, treatment, and clinical trials with both optimism and caution. A vision for how technology can make healthcare more human, not less — if we deploy it wisely."},
"Deep Work": {k: "In an economy that rewards focused, cognitively demanding work, most people are drowning in shallow tasks like email and social media. Newport argues that the ability to do deep work is becoming rare and therefore increasingly valuable. A practical guide to restructuring your work life around sustained concentration as a competitive advantage."},
"Demon Copperhead": {k: "A modern retelling of David Copperfield set in the opioid-ravaged Appalachian mountains, narrated by a sharp, funny foster kid named Demon. He survives abuse, addiction, and a system designed to fail kids like him, all told in his irresistible voice. A Pulitzer-winning novel that makes you feel the humanity behind the headlines of America's opioid crisis."},
"Don Quixote": {k: "An aging gentleman loses his mind reading chivalric romances and sets out as a knight-errant with his loyal squire Sancho Panza, tilting at windmills and seeing giants where there are none. It's the first modern novel — funny, heartbreaking, and endlessly layered. A story about the power of stories themselves, and whether it's nobler to see the world as it is or as it could be."},
"Dr. Jekyll and Mr. Hyde": {k: "A respected London doctor creates a potion that separates his good and evil natures into two distinct identities, losing control as Hyde grows stronger. The story explores the duality of human nature and the danger of suppressing our darker impulses rather than integrating them. A gothic classic that gave the English language one of its most enduring metaphors for the divided self."},
"Easy Pronunciation": {k: "A practical guide to improving English pronunciation through systematic practice of sounds, stress patterns, and intonation. Covers common trouble spots for non-native speakers with exercises and examples. A useful reference tool rather than a narrative — best used in short practice sessions."},
"Educated": {k: "Westover grew up in a survivalist family in Idaho with no formal schooling, a violent brother, and parents who distrusted medicine and government. Through self-teaching and sheer will, she earned a PhD from Cambridge. A stunning memoir about the transformative power of education and the painful cost of breaking from family."},
"Emma": {k: "Emma Woodhouse, wealthy and self-assured, fancies herself a matchmaker but consistently misreads everyone's romantic feelings — including her own. Her well-meaning meddling creates chaos in her small social circle until she's forced to confront her own blindness. Austen's wittiest comedy of manners, and a timeless portrait of how confidence without self-awareness leads to disaster."},
"Emotional Intelligence": {k: "Goleman argues that emotional intelligence — self-awareness, empathy, self-regulation, motivation, and social skills — matters more than IQ for success in life and work. He draws on neuroscience and psychology to show how EQ can be developed at any age. The book that launched a revolution in how we think about intelligence, leadership, and human potential."},
"Equanimity": {k: "A short guided meditation focused on finding calm acceptance of the present moment. Barouh walks you through releasing attachment to turbulent thoughts and emotions. A quick mindfulness exercise — good for a reset, not a deep dive."},
"Eugénie Grandet": {k: "In provincial France, a young woman's life is dominated by her miserly wealthy father, whose obsessive greed destroys everyone around him. When a dashing cousin visits, Eugénie experiences love for the first time — only to learn that love and money corrupt in equal measure. A sharp, devastating portrait of how avarice poisons family, romance, and an entire community."},
"Exit Interview with My Grandmother": {k: "A young woman reflects on adulthood through the prism of her relationship with her 92-year-old grandmother on the Upper West Side. Tender, observational, and full of intergenerational wisdom. A short, personal meditation on aging, family, and what we learn from the oldest people in our lives."},
"Fairy Tale": {k: "A teenager named Charlie befriends a reclusive old man and inherits a portal to a parallel world that's been overtaken by a dark force. Drawing on classic fairy tale structures, Charlie must become the hero this decaying fantasy kingdom needs. King's love letter to storytelling itself — a sprawling adventure about courage, sacrifice, and the endurance of good."},
"Fathers and Sons": {k: "A young nihilist named Bazarov visits the Russian countryside with his friend, clashing with the older generation over tradition, love, and the meaning of progress. Turgenev captures the eternal conflict between youth's desire to tear everything down and age's need to hold something together. A short, powerful novel about generational warfare that feels startlingly modern."},
"Fierce Love": {k: "From the author of Fierce Conversations, this book applies radical honesty and deep listening to romantic relationships. Scott argues that most relationships fail not from lack of love but from lack of real conversation. A guide to creating lasting love through courageous, truthful dialogue — one conversation at a time."},
"Greenwood": {k: "Spanning from 2038 to 1908, the novel follows a family's tangled history through old-growth forests, the Great Depression, and a climate-ravaged future. Each generation grapples with secrets, greed, and the tension between exploiting and protecting nature. An ambitious saga about how family legacies and environmental destruction are deeply intertwined."},
"Hamnet": {k: "A reimagining of the life of Shakespeare's wife Agnes and the death of their 11-year-old son Hamnet from plague in 1596. Agnes is portrayed as a fierce, unconventional healer while her husband is largely absent in London. A devastating, beautiful novel about grief, marriage, and how art can emerge from the deepest loss."},
"Hands-On Machine Learning": {k: "A comprehensive, practical guide to building machine learning systems using Scikit-Learn and TensorFlow, from basic concepts to deep neural networks. Géron emphasizes hands-on coding with real datasets alongside clear explanations of the underlying math. The go-to reference for practitioners who want to actually build ML systems, not just understand theory."},
"Have a Nice Day": {k: "Billy Crystal leads an all-star cast in a story about a former president desperately trying to put his affairs in order. Hilarious and poignant, it blends political satire with genuine emotion. A fun, well-performed audio production with Crystal at his charming best."},
"Herbie": {k: "Bestselling author Rich Cohen tells the story of a pivotal moment in his life — a college writing workshop that forced him to choose his path. A personal, reflective memoir about the intersection that determines who you become. Short and intimate, about creativity and the courage to commit to a life of writing."},
"Here One Moment": {k: "On a routine domestic flight, an ordinary-looking woman walks the aisle predicting each passenger's cause and age of death — and some predictions start coming true. The passengers must decide whether to accept fate or fight it while unraveling who this woman really is. A clever, moving novel about mortality, free will, and how knowing your ending changes how you live."},
"Hold Your Breath": {k: "A woman survives an attempted murder connected to a serial pattern: professional women drugged at bars and drowned in their bathtubs. Now she must figure out who's hunting her before he finishes the job. A tense, fast-paced novella-length thriller."},
"Holly": {k: "Holly Gibney, the unlikely detective from King's Bill Hodges trilogy, investigates the disappearance of a young woman during the COVID pandemic. The trail leads to a seemingly respectable retired couple hiding a monstrous secret. A character-driven thriller about evil hiding in plain sight, anchored by one of King's most beloved heroines."},
"Homo Deus": {k: "Having conquered famine, plague, and war, Harari asks what humanity will pursue next — and argues the answer is godlike powers: immortality, engineered happiness, and artificial superintelligence. He warns that these technologies could create a useless class of humans replaced by algorithms. A provocative, sweeping vision of the future that challenges every assumption about human purpose."},
"How to Achieve Financial Independence and Retire Early": {k: "Roth lays out the mechanics of the FIRE movement: savings rate as the dominant variable, the 4% withdrawal rule, and the arithmetic linking spending to years until independence. He frames early retirement less as escape from work than as buying back control of your time. A compact lecture course on the numbers behind a much-hyped idea."},
"How to Day Trade for a Living": {k: "Aziz covers the mechanics of intraday trading: platforms, order types, risk limits, and the handful of setups he trades. Much of the book is about discipline and position sizing rather than strategy. Worth reading with the base rates in mind — most retail day traders lose money, which the book acknowledges more briefly than the title suggests."},
"How to Make a Plant Love You": {k: "Oakes, who grows 1,000 plants in her Brooklyn apartment, shares practical advice and the science behind keeping houseplants thriving. Beyond care tips, she explores the psychological and health benefits of living with plants. A warm guide for anyone who wants to bring nature indoors and actually keep it alive."},
"How to Talk to Anyone": {k: "Lowndes offers 92 practical techniques for making strong first impressions, mastering small talk, and building instant rapport. Tips range from body language to conversation strategies for networking and dating. A useful toolbox of social skills, best taken as a buffet of ideas rather than a rigid system."},
"I Capture the Castle": {k: "Seventeen-year-old Cassandra Mortmain journals about life in a crumbling English castle with her eccentric, broke family — including a father with writer's block and a glamorous older sister. When two wealthy American brothers enter their lives, everything changes. A witty, enchanting coming-of-age novel about first love, creativity, and growing up in genteel poverty."},
"I'm Fine and Neither Are You": {k: "After her seemingly perfect best friend dies suddenly, Penelope discovers the woman's life was falling apart behind closed doors. Shaken, she makes a radical pact with her husband: total honesty for 30 days, no matter the cost. A relatable novel about the gap between the lives we perform and the ones we actually live."},
"In Defense of Food": {k: "Pollan's simple manifesto: eat food, not too much, mostly plants. He argues that the Western diet's focus on nutrients rather than whole foods has made us sicker. A clear-eyed critique of the food industry and nutritional science that makes healthy eating feel refreshingly simple."},
"Inside Jobs": {k: "Three offbeat crime stories set during quarantine from the Edgar Award-winning author. Each tale blends mystery with the surreal circumstances of pandemic life. Clever, entertaining short fiction that captures the strangeness of 2020."},
"Intermezzo": {k: "Two very different brothers — Peter, a successful but emotionally wrecked lawyer, and Ivan, a socially awkward chess prodigy — grieve their father's death while navigating complicated love lives. Rooney explores how grief can either destroy or deepen the bonds between people. Her most mature, emotionally generous novel yet, about family as the relationship you can't choose but can't escape."},
"Intimations: Six Essays": {k: "Written during early COVID lockdown, Smith reflects on the pandemic's exposure of inequality, the strange suspension of normal life, and what we owe each other. Six spare, elegant essays that capture a specific historical moment with lasting insight. A slim but powerful collection about privilege, mortality, and the stories we tell during crisis."},
"It Burns": {k: "An audio documentary about the scandal-plagued subculture of competitive chilli breeding, where growers race to create the world's hottest pepper. Rivalries, cheating, and obsession drive people to extremes over capsaicin levels. A surprisingly gripping niche documentary about passion, competition, and the humans behind an absurd pursuit."},
"It's OK to Be Angry About Capitalism": {k: "Sanders makes his case that oligarchic capitalism has rigged the American economy to benefit billionaires while crushing the working class. He proposes transformational policies including Medicare for All, free college, and a living wage. A fiery progressive manifesto — part diagnosis of systemic inequality, part blueprint for democratic socialism."},
"Just Mercy": {k: "Stevenson, a lawyer who founded the Equal Justice Initiative, fights to free Walter McMillian — a Black man wrongly convicted of murder in Alabama. Along the way, he confronts a justice system riddled with racial bias, prosecutorial misconduct, and indifference to the poor. A devastating, hopeful book about mercy, injustice, and the belief that each person is more than the worst thing they've done."},
"Killer by Nature": {k: "An audio drama exploring the eternal question of nature vs. nurture through a compelling crime narrative. Are some people born evil, or does life make them that way? A well-acted British production that keeps you thinking after it ends."},
"Lab Girl": {k: "Jahren interweaves her life as a scientist — building labs, scrounging for grants, studying trees and seeds — with luminous passages about how plants grow, survive, and adapt. Her deep friendship with her eccentric lab partner Bill anchors the human story. A memoir that makes you see both science and nature with completely new eyes."},
"Lady Tan's Circle of Women": {k: "In 15th-century China, Lady Tan defies convention to become one of the few female physicians, navigating foot-binding, arranged marriage, and patriarchal medicine. She uses her knowledge to heal women in secret while balancing duty to her husband's family. A richly detailed historical novel about female solidarity and the quiet power of women's knowledge passed through generations."},
"Life Ever After": {k: "In a tech-obsessed future city, a woman and man meet in a waiting room before a procedure that promises to merge science and technology with their minds and bodies. The story questions whether eternal life through technology is a gift or a curse. A thought-provoking sci-fi audio drama about what we lose when we try to optimize away death."},
"Mad Honey": {k: "Two families' lives collide when a teenager is found dead, and her boyfriend — the son of a beekeeper starting over after domestic abuse — is accused. Told in alternating perspectives, the story peels back layers of secrets about identity, violence, and who we really are. A suspenseful novel with a powerful twist about transgender identity and the danger of assumptions."},
"Maid": {k: "A single mother works as a house cleaner while navigating poverty, domestic violence, government assistance, and the shame attached to all of it. Land reveals how the system traps people in cycles of scarcity while demanding they remain grateful. A visceral memoir about the dignity of hard work and the impossible math of surviving on minimum wage in America."},
"Man's Search for Meaning": {k: "Frankl, a psychiatrist who survived Auschwitz, argues that humans can endure almost anything if they find meaning in their suffering. He developed logotherapy — the idea that the primary drive in life is not pleasure but purpose. One of the most important books of the 20th century about what keeps people alive when everything is taken from them.", w: "Short, devastating, and universally transformative. A psychiatrist who survived Auschwitz on finding purpose through suffering. You'll think about it for years."},
"Martyr!": {k: "Cyrus Shams, the orphaned, newly sober son of Iranian immigrants, becomes obsessed with the concept of meaningful death — from martyrs to artists who died young. His search leads him to a dying painter in the Brooklyn Museum who challenges everything he believes. A lyrical, funny debut novel about grief, addiction, and whether a beautiful death can give meaning to a messy life."},
"Mating in Captivity": {k: "Perel tackles the paradox at the heart of long-term relationships: we want security and passion, but they work against each other. She argues that desire needs mystery and separateness — the very things domesticity erodes. A provocative rethinking of how to maintain erotic energy in committed relationships by embracing, not eliminating, distance."},
"Maybe You Should Talk to Someone": {k: "A therapist in crisis seeks her own therapist, and the parallel stories of her patients and her own journey reveal universal truths about change, loss, and connection. Gottlieb shows that therapy works not through advice but through the relationship itself. A warm, wise, often funny book that normalizes getting help and illuminates the messy process of becoming unstuck."},
"Medical Bondage": {k: "Owens documents how American gynecology was built on the exploitation of enslaved Black women who were experimented on without consent or anesthesia. She reveals how 19th-century ideas about race and gender shaped medical practice in ways that persist today. An essential, disturbing history that explains the roots of racial bias in modern healthcare."},
"Midnight Son": {k: "An Alaska Native writer and musician investigates a real-life mystery that pits Native American folklore against the US justice system. A deeply personal documentary that blends true crime with cultural exploration. Atmospheric and haunting — about justice, identity, and whose stories get told."},
"Mind Reading 2.0": {k: "A Hidden Brain episode exploring the psychology of how we try to understand what others are thinking and feeling. Very short clip — essentially a teaser or sample."},
"Modern Romance": {k: "Ansari teams up with a sociologist to investigate how technology has transformed dating — from texting anxiety to the paradox of too many choices on apps. He travels the world comparing how different cultures approach modern love and commitment. A funny, research-backed look at why finding a partner today feels both easier and more overwhelming than ever."},
"My Lost Family": {k: "A true story: a teenage Lillian marries a charming older man in 1950s London, and one day he takes their children to the park and never returns. Decades later, the mystery of the vanished family begins to unravel. A gripping real-life mystery about abandonment, identity, and the long search for truth."},
"My Name Is Mary Sutter": {k: "During the Civil War, a brilliant young midwife from Albany fights to become a surgeon in an era when women were barred from medicine. She leaves heartbreak behind and heads to Washington, where she confronts the brutal reality of battlefield hospitals. A story of determination, sacrifice, and one woman's refusal to accept the limits placed on her."},
"Never Split the Difference": {k: "A former FBI hostage negotiator reveals the psychological tactics he used in life-or-death negotiations and shows how they apply to everyday life. Key techniques include tactical empathy, calibrated questions, and the power of no. A practical, story-driven guide to negotiation that works for salary talks, business deals, and even family arguments.", w: "Immediately applicable to work, negotiation, and daily life. Techniques from an FBI hostage negotiator that you'll use the same week you finish it."},
"Normal People": {k: "Connell and Marianne circle each other from secondary school in small-town Ireland through university at Trinity College Dublin, their relationship shifting between intimacy and distance. Class differences, insecurity, and miscommunication keep pulling them apart even as they remain deeply connected. A quiet, devastating novel about how the people who know us best can still feel impossibly far away.", w: "Only 7.5 hours, emotionally precise, and you already have Intermezzo waiting. A quick, powerful listen about love and miscommunication."},
"Olio Live": {k: "A live performance of Jess's Pulitzer Prize-winning poetry collection celebrating African American artists and musicians from the post-Civil War era. The poems interweave historical voices with experimental forms. A powerful, performed literary experience rather than a traditional audiobook."},
"Olive Kitteridge": {k: "Through interconnected stories set in a small Maine coastal town, we meet Olive — a retired math teacher who is blunt, difficult, sometimes cruel, and deeply human. Each story reveals a different facet of her character and the lives she touches and disrupts. A Pulitzer-winning portrait of ordinary life that is devastatingly perceptive about loneliness, aging, and love's imperfect forms."},
"On Edge": {k: "A science journalist diagnosed with anxiety at 20 traces both her personal experience and the neuroscience behind anxiety disorders. She explores how genetics, brain chemistry, and life events conspire to create chronic anxiety. A blend of memoir and medical journalism that destigmatizes anxiety while showing how much science still doesn't understand."},
"One Hundred Years of Solitude": {k: "Seven generations of the Buendía family rise and fall in the fictional Colombian town of Macondo, trapped in cycles of love, war, obsession, and solitude. Magical realism blurs the line between the extraordinary and the mundane as history repeats itself in haunting patterns. One of literature's greatest achievements — a novel about time, memory, and the human compulsion to repeat our mistakes."},
"One-on-One with Eric Topol": {k: "Medscape editor interviews leading experts on clinical and scientific developments."},
"Out of Africa": {k: "Danish author Karen Blixen's memoir of running a coffee plantation in Kenya in the early 20th century, when Africa was still a romantic and formidable continent to Europeans. She writes about the land, the Kikuyu and Maasai people, and her love affair with Denys Finch Hatton. A lyrical, elegiac classic — beautiful prose, though its colonial perspective deserves critical reading today."},
"Outliers": {k: "Success isn't just about individual talent — it's shaped by timing, culture, family, and accumulated advantages like the famous 10,000 hours of practice. Gladwell examines everyone from hockey players to Bill Gates to show how hidden structural factors create outliers. A paradigm-shifting look at achievement that makes you question the myth of the self-made person."},
"Pachinko": {k: "Four generations of a Korean family navigate life in 20th-century Japan, where they remain permanent outsiders no matter how long they stay. Beginning with Sunja's pregnancy in a fishing village and following her descendants through war, poverty, and the pachinko parlors that sustain them. Read by Sandra Oh; a National Book Award finalist about dignity under sustained discrimination."},
"Pont Neuf": {k: "A young American woman arrives in France in 1944, determined to be a war correspondent alongside Martha Gellhorn and Ernest Hemingway. As she finds her own voice as a writer, she navigates the egos of literary giants and the chaos of liberation-era Paris. A vivid novella about ambition, war, and a woman refusing to be anyone's footnote."},
"Poverty, by America": {k: "Desmond argues that poverty in America persists not despite the wealthy but because of them — through exploitation, tax policy, and segregation. He shows how middle-class and rich Americans benefit from systems that keep millions poor. A sharp, uncomfortable book that reframes poverty as something we all participate in, not just a problem they have."},
"Power and Prediction": {k: "The authors of Prediction Machines return to examine how AI's prediction capability will reshape decision-making across industries and society. They argue we're at a tipping point where AI moves from tool to system-level disruptor. A framework for understanding the economic transformation AI is about to unleash."},
"Professional Troublemaker": {k: "Ajayi Jones argues that the world needs more professional troublemakers — people willing to speak truth, challenge norms, and fight fear. She shares personal stories of times she chose courage over comfort, from speaking up at work to confronting injustice. A funny, fiery guide to being bold in a world that rewards silence and conformity."},
"Project Hail Mary": {k: "A lone astronaut wakes on a spacecraft with amnesia and two dead crewmates, and must reconstruct both his memory and his mission before the sun dims and Earth dies. Weir applies the problem-solving rhythm of The Martian to a much stranger premise, including a first-contact friendship. Ray Porter's narration is widely considered one of the best in audio."},
"Projections": {k: "A pioneering neuroscientist and psychiatrist uses clinical stories to illuminate how the brain creates emotion, memory, and consciousness. Deisseroth invented optogenetics — using light to control brain cells — and connects his research to real patients' struggles. A poetic, deeply human exploration of what makes us feel, grieve, love, and lose our minds."},
"Proof of Love": {k: "A tragic accident splinters an upper-class Black family and forces a woman to confront uncomfortable truths about her marriage. Explores how crisis reveals the cracks in relationships we thought were solid. A short, sharp audio drama about love, race, and self-deception."},
"Rivals! Frenemies Who Changed the World": {k: "Entertaining stories of historical rivalries — like the two men whose hatred of each other led to most of our dinosaur fossil discoveries. Each episode shows how competition and personal grudges accidentally drove progress. A fun, Monty Python-esque history listen perfect for kids and adults."},
"Sapiens": {k: "Harari traces the entire arc of human history — from the cognitive revolution 70,000 years ago through agricultural, scientific, and industrial revolutions to today. His key argument: Homo sapiens conquered the world through our unique ability to believe in shared fictions like money, nations, and religion. A sweeping, provocative book that makes you question every assumption about why human civilization looks the way it does."},
"Sea Wall / A Life": {k: "Two monologues performed by Jake Gyllenhaal and Tom Sturridge — one about a photographer dealing with devastating loss, the other about a man becoming a father. Both explore how the biggest moments in life are impossible to articulate yet must be spoken. Short, emotionally devastating theater that hits harder than most full-length novels."},
"Secrets of a Passionate Marriage": {k: "Schnarch presents a revolutionary approach to intimacy based on differentiation — the ability to maintain your sense of self while staying close to your partner. He argues that the best sex and deepest connection come from personal growth, not technique. A challenging, paradigm-shifting take on why most relationship advice gets intimacy backwards."},
"Serendipity": {k: "Best friends Hero and Sunday are on holiday in New York when Sunday convinces the conservative Hero to adopt the wild persona of Lola. What starts as a game spirals into real consequences involving love, mistaken identity, and spectacular misunderstandings. A light, comedic romance about stepping outside your comfort zone."},
"Slaughterhouse-Five": {k: "Billy Pilgrim becomes unstuck in time, experiencing moments from his life out of order — including the firebombing of Dresden as a POW and his abduction by aliens from Tralfamadore. Vonnegut uses dark humor and science fiction to process the trauma of war and the absurdity of human violence. An anti-war classic that is funny, devastating, and unlike anything else."},
"Smart Couples Finish Rich": {k: "Bach provides a practical framework for couples to align on money — from identifying your values to automating savings to planning for retirement together. The key insight: money fights are usually about values, not dollars. A solid financial planning guide for couples that's actionable even if some specific advice is dated."},
"So Good They Can't Ignore You": {k: "Newport argues that follow your passion is bad career advice — instead, build rare and valuable skills and the passion will follow. He introduces the concept of career capital and shows how craftsman mindset beats passion mindset. A contrarian, evidence-based guide to building a career you love through deliberate practice rather than soul-searching."},
"Stephen Fry's Victorian Secrets": {k: "Beneath the prim Victorian surface lay an era of scandal, sexual obsession, medical horrors, and social hypocrisy that makes modern life seem tame. Narrated by Stephen Fry, the series digs into the bizarre, dark, and fascinating underbelly of the 19th century. An entertaining romp through history's most repressed — and secretly wild — era."},
"Stranger in the Woods": {k: "A photographer takes a dream job with wealthy clients in a remote mountain estate, but their behavior becomes increasingly strange and unsettling. As she digs deeper, she discovers the family is hiding something dangerous in the surrounding forest. A suspenseful thriller about isolation, paranoia, and realizing too late that you're trapped."},
"Strangers to Ourselves": {k: "Aviv profiles people whose lives have been shaped by psychiatric diagnoses, including her own childhood hospitalization for anorexia at age six. She questions how the stories we're given about our mental illness become our identity. A nuanced exploration of how diagnosis can both illuminate and imprison, and how culture shapes what we call mental health."},
"Talking to Strangers": {k: "Gladwell explores why we're so bad at reading people we don't know — from CIA agents fooled by double agents to police encounters that turn fatal. He introduces concepts like default to truth and coupling to explain systematic misjudgment. A provocative argument that our tools for understanding strangers are fundamentally broken, with real consequences."},
"The 3-Day Effect": {k: "Williams investigates the science behind why spending three days in nature measurably improves happiness, creativity, and health. She joins military veterans on a river trip to document the neurological and psychological shifts that occur. A compelling, research-backed argument for why we all need more wild time — not just a walk in the park, but real immersion."},
"The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People": {k: "Covey's framework moves from dependence to independence to interdependence through seven principles: be proactive, begin with the end in mind, put first things first, think win-win, seek to understand, synergize, and sharpen the saw. The core message is character-based effectiveness over quick-fix personality techniques. A foundational personal development book that remains relevant decades later."},
"The Adrenal Thyroid Revolution": {k: "Dr. Romm addresses how chronic stress, inflammation, and hormonal imbalance — particularly in women — create a cascade of symptoms doctors often dismiss or medicate without investigation. She provides a 28-day program combining nutrition, supplements, and lifestyle changes. A holistic guide to understanding the stress-thyroid-adrenal connection and taking back your health."},
"The Alchemist": {k: "A young Andalusian shepherd named Santiago travels from Spain to Egypt in pursuit of a recurring dream about treasure near the Pyramids. Along the way, he learns to read omens, discovers his Personal Legend, and finds that the journey matters more than the destination. A simple, parable-like novel about following your dreams that resonates deeply with some readers and feels too simplistic to others."},
"The Alignment Problem": {k: "Christian examines how machine learning systems absorb and amplify human biases, and the ongoing struggle to align AI with human values. He traces the problem from early neural networks to modern deep learning, showing where things go wrong. A crucial book about the most important unsolved problem in AI — making machines that actually do what we want."},
"The Anatomy of Peace": {k: "Through a fictional retreat for parents of troubled teens, the book explores how we unconsciously create conflict by seeing others as objects rather than people. The key insight is that most interpersonal problems stem from a heart at war rather than from the other person. A framework for resolving conflict that starts with changing how you see people, not how you act."},
"The Awkward Thoughts of W. Kamau Bell": {k: "The comedian and CNN host shares essays on race, fatherhood, politics, and being a Black nerd navigating America's contradictions. He moves between sharp social commentary and self-deprecating humor with genuine warmth. An honest, funny memoir about trying to be a good person in a complicated world while not taking yourself too seriously."},
"The Body Keeps the Score": {k: "Van der Kolk, a leading trauma researcher, shows how trauma literally reshapes the body and brain, and that traditional talk therapy alone often isn't enough. He explores innovative treatments including EMDR, yoga, neurofeedback, and theater. A groundbreaking, essential book for understanding how trauma lives in the body and how to finally release it.", w: "Pairs perfectly with Projections and The Boy Who Was Raised as a Dog. The most influential trauma book of the last 20 years. Relevant to virtually every healthcare field."},
"The Book of Why": {k: "Pearl argues that statistics alone can never establish causation — you need causal models and a ladder of causation from observation to intervention to counterfactuals. He shows how this framework revolutionizes AI, medicine, and social science. A paradigm-shifting book that makes the case for why 'why' is the most important question science can ask.", w: "The causal inference framework is foundational for data science + medicine. Worth pushing through - it clicks once you get the ladder of causation."},
"The Book on Rental Property Investing": {k: "Turner covers buy-and-hold real estate end to end: deal analysis, financing, tenant screening, and the arithmetic of cash flow versus appreciation. Heavy on worked numbers and rules of thumb from the BiggerPockets school. Useful as a mechanics reference, though written from inside an ecosystem that sells real estate optimism."},
"The Botany of Desire": {k: "Pollan examines four plants — apple, tulip, marijuana, and potato — and how each evolved to satisfy a human desire: sweetness, beauty, intoxication, and control. He flips the script, arguing plants have been using us as much as we've been using them. A fascinating look at the co-evolutionary dance between humans and the natural world."},
"The Boy Who Was Raised as a Dog": {k: "A child psychiatrist shares cases of children who survived extreme trauma — neglect, abuse, cult captivity — and reveals how early experiences physically shape the developing brain. Perry shows that healing requires patterned, repetitive, nurturing experiences, not just talk therapy. A paradigm-shifting book about childhood trauma that will change how you think about behavior and resilience."},
"The Business of Botanicals": {k: "Armbrecht traces the global herbal supplement industry from wild-harvested plants to store shelves, visiting farms and factories across continents. She reveals the tension between traditional plant medicine and industrialized production driven by profit. A thoughtful examination of whether the booming wellness industry can truly honor the plants and people behind it."},
"The Correspondent": {k: "An epistolary novel told through the letters of Sybil Van Antwerp, a retired legal clerk in her seventies writing to family, strangers, and famous authors. Slowly the correspondence circles a decision in her past she has never faced directly. A large cast of narrators gives each correspondent a distinct voice."},
"The Dead Drink First": {k: "Maharidge's WWII veteran father never spoke about the war, but behind closed doors, even mundane moments could trigger violent episodes of untreated PTSD. A son's attempt to understand generational trauma through his father's silence. A raw, honest memoir about how war follows soldiers home and into their children's lives."},
"The DevOps Handbook": {k: "A comprehensive guide to implementing DevOps practices that enable faster software delivery, better reliability, and healthier engineering culture. Covers continuous integration, automated testing, infrastructure as code, and organizational transformation. Essential reading for anyone in tech who wants to understand how high-performing teams actually ship software."},
"The Digital Doctor": {k: "Wachter chronicles how electronic health records and digital tools have transformed medicine — often not for the better. He documents the unintended consequences: alert fatigue, depersonalized care, and new categories of medical errors. A balanced assessment of healthcare IT that shows why the digital revolution in medicine has been so much harder than expected."},
"The Emperor of All Maladies": {k: "Mukherjee writes a sweeping biography of cancer from ancient Egypt to modern immunotherapy, interweaving science, politics, and patient stories. He traces how our understanding evolved from radical surgeries to targeted therapies, and the personalities who drove each breakthrough. A Pulitzer-winning masterpiece that makes you understand cancer as both a biological and cultural phenomenon."},
"The Forty Rules of Love": {k: "A bored Massachusetts housewife reads a novel about the 13th-century meeting of Rumi and the wandering dervish Shams of Tabriz, and her own life begins to transform. The parallel stories explore how divine love can shatter convention and awaken the soul. A sweeping novel about spiritual awakening, the courage to change, and love as a force that transcends time.", w: "Connects to your cultural background, spiritual and beautiful. A novel about Rumi and Shams that interweaves past and present."},
"The Great Gatsby": {k: "Jay Gatsby, a mysterious millionaire, throws lavish parties on Long Island in pursuit of Daisy Buchanan, the woman he lost years ago. Narrated by his neighbor Nick Carraway, the story exposes the hollowness behind the glittering American Dream of the 1920s. The quintessential American novel about wealth, love, illusion, and the impossibility of recapturing the past."},
"The Kite Runner": {k: "Amir and Hassan grow up together in Kabul — one a wealthy Pashtun, the other a Hazara servant's son — bound by a friendship shattered by betrayal and cowardice during a kite-flying tournament. Decades later, Amir returns to Taliban-controlled Afghanistan seeking redemption. A devastating story about guilt, loyalty, and whether it's ever possible to be good again."},
"The Lean Startup": {k: "Ries reframes a startup as an experiment engine: build a minimum viable product, measure real user behavior, and pivot or persevere on the evidence. The core claim is that most startup failure is preventable waste — building carefully the wrong thing. Its vocabulary (MVP, pivot, validated learning) became the default language of the 2010s tech industry."},
"The Loneliness of Sonia and Sunny": {k: "Two young people from an Indian family — one in India, one in America — navigate love, identity, and the pull of tradition across continents and decades. Desai explores how immigration fractures families and how modernity clashes with inherited expectations. An epic novel from the Booker Prize winner about belonging nowhere and everywhere at once."},
"The Marriage Portrait": {k: "In 1561 Florence, young Lucrezia de' Medici is married off to the Duke of Ferrara and gradually realizes he may intend to kill her. Alternating between her childhood as an overlooked artist and her dangerous married life, the tension builds relentlessly. A lush historical thriller about a woman's fight for survival against the power structures designed to erase her."},
"The Method": {k: "A frustrated actor learns about Method acting and dives deep — landing the lead role as a serial killer in a TV series. But the deeper he goes into the character, the more the line between performance and reality dissolves. A creepy, fast-paced audio thriller about how pretending to be someone else can consume who you really are."},
"The New York Times Digest": {k: "Daily news digest."},
"The Nose": {k: "A Russian official wakes up one morning to find his nose has left his face and is walking around St. Petersburg in a higher-ranking uniform. His desperate attempts to reclaim it are met with bureaucratic absurdity. A surreal, hilarious satire about status, identity, and the madness of social hierarchy — Gogol at his most brilliantly weird."},
"The Ocean at the End of the Lane": {k: "A man returns to his childhood home and remembers a terrifying supernatural event involving his neighbor Lettie Hempstock and her mysterious family. As a boy, he stumbled into an ancient battle between primordial forces, with only the Hempstock women standing between him and oblivion. A dark, dreamlike fable about memory, childhood fear, and the magic that protects us when we're small."},
"The Odyssey": {k: "Odysseus spends ten years trying to get home from Troy, obstructed by gods, monsters, and his own appetite for risk, while Penelope holds off suitors in Ithaca. Wilson's translation is the first into English by a woman and strips away centuries of ornamental padding for a fast, plain iambic line. Narrated by Claire Danes, it plays as story rather than monument."},
"The Overcoat": {k: "A lowly, mocked St. Petersburg clerk saves desperately for a new overcoat, and when it's finally stolen, the bureaucracy offers no help. After his death from despair, his ghost haunts the city stealing overcoats from the powerful. A tragicomic masterpiece about poverty, dignity, and the brutal indifference of systems to individual suffering."},
"The Overstory": {k: "Nine Americans whose lives are shaped by trees gradually converge into a collective story of ecological activism, from peaceful protest to radical action. Powers makes trees the true protagonists — organisms that communicate, cooperate, and endure on timescales humans can barely comprehend. A Pulitzer-winning novel that may permanently change how you see forests, time, and humanity's place in the natural world."},
"The Picture of Dorian Gray": {k: "A beautiful young man wishes that his portrait would age instead of him — and gets his wish, freeing him to pursue a life of hedonism while his painting absorbs every sin. As Dorian's cruelty deepens, the portrait becomes a horrifying record of his soul. Wilde's only novel — a gothic meditation on beauty, morality, and the cost of living without consequences."},
"The Power of Habit": {k: "Habits work through a loop: cue, routine, reward — and you can change any habit by keeping the cue and reward but swapping the routine. Duhigg explores how this applies from individual behavior to organizational culture to social movements. A foundational book on behavior change with compelling stories from Alcoa to the civil rights movement."},
"The Power of Now": {k: "Tolle argues that almost all human suffering comes from identification with the mind and its compulsive thinking — especially about past and future. Presence, not thought, is the key to peace and awakening. A spiritual classic that's either life-changing or frustrating depending on where you are when you read it."},
"The Power of Self-Compassion": {k: "Cameron provides practical tools — meditations, exercises, journaling — to build mindfulness and self-compassion as daily habits. The goal is replacing self-criticism with self-kindness as your default response to difficulty. A guided program for anyone who's harder on themselves than they would be on a friend."},
"The Prophet": {k: "A wise man about to leave the city where he's lived for 12 years is asked by the people to share his wisdom on love, marriage, children, work, freedom, and death. Each topic receives a lyrical, almost scriptural meditation. A beloved, timeless book of philosophical poetry that reads like sacred text — short enough to read in one sitting, deep enough to revisit for a lifetime."},
"The Psychology of Money": {k: "Housel argues that financial success is more about behavior than intelligence — how you handle money matters more than what you know about it. Through 19 short stories, he explores how luck, risk, ego, and personal history shape financial decisions. A refreshingly human take on money that's less about spreadsheets and more about psychology."},
"The Red and the Black": {k: "Julien Sorel, an ambitious young man in post-Napoleonic France, uses intelligence and seduction to climb the social ladder from a provincial town to Parisian high society. His hunger for status collides with genuine passion, leading to a dramatic downfall. A foundational novel of psychological realism about ambition, class, and the war between calculation and feeling."},
"The Roommate": {k: "A young teacher wakes to find her roommate missing and a detective at the door, and her life quickly spirals out of control. A prequel novella to the Cormac Reilly crime series set in Ireland. A tight, suspenseful short listen that works as a standalone thriller."},
"The Rosie Effect": {k: "Don Tillman is back, now married to Rosie and living in New York — but a surprise pregnancy throws his carefully structured life into chaos. His logical approach to preparing for fatherhood creates hilarious misunderstandings and nearly destroys his marriage. A warm, funny sequel about how love requires flexibility, not just optimization."},
"The Rosie Project": {k: "Don Tillman, a socially awkward genetics professor, creates a scientifically rigorous questionnaire to find the perfect wife — then meets Rosie, who fails every criterion. As they search for Rosie's biological father, Don discovers that love doesn't follow logic. A charming, funny novel about neurodiversity, connection, and letting go of the need to control everything."},
"The Ruin": {k: "Twenty years after young detective Cormac Reilly found two neglected children and their mother's body, the case resurfaces when the surviving boy dies in an apparent suicide. His girlfriend doesn't believe it, and Reilly — now back in Galway — is drawn back in. A gripping Irish crime thriller about cold cases, institutional failure, and justice delayed."},
"The Safekeep": {k: "In 1960s Netherlands, uptight Isabel guards her late mother's house with obsessive control until her brother's free-spirited girlfriend moves in and disrupts everything. As objects start disappearing, Isabel's suspicion gives way to unexpected desire and buried truths about the house's wartime history. A taut novel about post-war guilt, repression, and what we inherit from our families' silences."},
"The Searcher": {k: "A retired Chicago cop moves to a remote Irish village seeking peace, but gets drawn into a missing-person case by a local teenager. The investigation unravels the village's tightly kept secrets and tests how far he'll go for justice. A slow-burn literary thriller about reinvention, community, and moral obligation."},
"The Sense of Style": {k: "Pinker uses cognitive science and linguistics to explain why so much writing is bad and how to make it better. He advocates classic style — writing as if showing something to the reader — over academic jargon and bureaucratic hedging. A modern, evidence-based style guide that treats good writing as clear thinking made visible."},
"The Signature of All Things": {k: "Alma Whittaker, born in 1800 to a wealthy botanical family in Philadelphia, becomes a brilliant researcher studying mosses. Her intellectual journey parallels Darwin's, as she grapples with love, loss, and the mystery of evolution. An epic novel spanning the 19th century about science, desire, and a woman's relentless pursuit of understanding."},
"The Simple Path to Wealth": {k: "Collins distills investing to a few durable ideas: avoid debt, spend less than you earn, and put the surplus in low-cost total-market index funds. Written originally as letters to his daughter, it argues that complexity in personal finance mostly serves the people selling it. A short, plainspoken case for the simplest strategy being the one that actually works."},
"The Sisters": {k: "A prequel to The Ruin set 10 years earlier, following young detective Carrie Ryan at the start of her career. She encounters a case that tests her instincts and her idealism. A tight Irish crime novella that adds depth to the Cormac Reilly series."},
"The Space Within": {k: "Seven-year-old Sophie goes missing for six hours, and this audio drama explores what happened through multiple perspectives. A full-cast production starring Jessica Chastain and Bobby Cannavale. A tense, emotionally layered story about a family in crisis."},
"The Spectator Bird": {k: "Retired literary agent Joe Allston, stuck in quiet old age in California, receives a letter that triggers memories of a transformative trip to Denmark decades earlier. As he and his wife revisit his old journal, buried secrets about desire and regret surface. A reflective, elegant novel about aging, marriage, and the stories we tell ourselves about who we were."},
"The Tea Shoppe": {k: "Eleanor, the manager of a cozy English tea shop, finds peace in the ritual of making Masala Chai and the beauty of her village during the holidays. A gentle, atmospheric short story about the comfort of routine and place. A warm listen for when you want something soothing and brief."},
"The Wall Street Journal Digest": {k: "Daily news digest."},
"The Washington Post Digest": {k: "Daily news digest."},
"The Weight of Ink": {k: "In modern London, a historian discovers 17th-century documents written by a Jewish woman serving as a scribe for a blind rabbi. The dual timeline reveals the scribe's secret intellectual life during the plague years and the historian's own academic battles. A rich novel about women's silenced voices, the power of writing, and what history chooses to remember."},
"The Woman in Me": {k: "Spears tells her own story for the first time — the meteoric rise, the public breakdown, and the 13-year conservatorship controlled by her father. She reveals the manipulation, isolation, and loss of autonomy she endured while the world watched and judged. A raw, emotional memoir about reclaiming your voice after it's been taken from you."},
"The Year of Magical Thinking": {k: "Didion's memoir adapted as a one-woman play about the year following her husband's sudden death and her daughter's critical illness. She dissects grief with surgical precision while revealing its irrational, magical logic. A devastating examination of how loss deranges the mind and how we try to think our way through the unthinkable."},
"Think and Grow Rich": {k: "Hill distills interviews with early 20th-century titans like Carnegie and Ford into 13 principles for achieving wealth and success. The core idea is that desire, faith, and persistence — channeled through a definite plan — can overcome any obstacle. A foundational self-help classic, though dated in style, that emphasizes the power of mindset and focused intention."},
"Thinking about Capitalism": {k: "A comprehensive lecture series tracing the intellectual history of capitalism from Adam Smith through Marx, Schumpeter, Hayek, and beyond. Muller examines how thinkers across centuries have debated the benefits and harms of market economies. A rigorous, balanced overview ideal for understanding the philosophical foundations of the economic system we live in."},
"Three Deaths": {k: "Three beings die in this short story — a wealthy noblewoman, a poor coachman, and a tree — each facing death in radically different ways. Tolstoy contrasts the noblewoman's denial and fear with the peasant's acceptance and the tree's silent, natural passing. A brief, profound meditation on mortality, faith, and the relationship between how we live and how we die."},
"Tinaca Jones": {k: "Retta stars as the unforgettable Tinaca Jones in this dramatic comedy about a woman navigating life's absurdities with humor and strength. A character-driven audio original with a memorable lead performance. Light, entertaining, and carried by Retta's natural charisma."},
"Tradeoffs": {k: "Healthcare policy podcast exploring tough choices in cost, equity, and access."},
"Treasure Island": {k: "Young Jim Hawkins discovers a treasure map and sets sail, only to find that the ship's cook Long John Silver is a pirate planning mutiny. The classic adventure tale of buried treasure, betrayal, and a boy's coming-of-age on the high seas. A beautifully produced Audible adaptation of one of literature's greatest adventure stories."},
"Uncaring": {k: "A former Kaiser Permanente CEO exposes how physician culture — hierarchy, autonomy worship, and resistance to change — harms both patients and doctors. He argues that systemic problems are rooted in medical training and professional identity, not individual bad actors. A provocative insider critique of why American healthcare is broken at a cultural level."},
"Vision to Reality": {k: "Jenkins offers a framework for turning personal and professional visions into achievable reality through goal-setting, mindset shifts, and strategic action. A practical self-help guide for people who want to stop working aimlessly and start building toward something meaningful. Motivational and structured — for when you need a push to move from dreaming to doing."},
"We Run the Tides": {k: "Teenage Eulabee and her magnetic best friend Maria Fabiola rule the streets of Sea Cliff, San Francisco, until a lie about a witnessed crime tears them apart. As the neighborhood gentrifies and friendships fracture, Eulabee must navigate loyalty, truth, and identity. A sharp coming-of-age novel about how a single moment can reshape an entire life."},
"Weapons of Math Destruction": {k: "O'Neil exposes how algorithms used in hiring, lending, policing, and education encode bias and punish the poor while remaining opaque and unaccountable. These weapons of math destruction create feedback loops that widen inequality under a veneer of objectivity. A wake-up call about how big data can reinforce the very injustices it claims to solve."},
"When You Finish Saving the World": {k: "Three members of the same family each try to connect with someone who doesn't quite see them — a mother, a father, and a teenage son, all talking past each other. Eisenberg's debut explores the gap between wanting to do good and actually understanding the people closest to you. A sharp, well-performed audio drama about family, ego, and the limits of empathy."},
"Where the Crawdads Sing": {k: "Kya Clark, abandoned by her family, raises herself in the marshes of North Carolina, becoming an expert naturalist while the town shuns her as the Marsh Girl. When a local man is found dead, she becomes the prime suspect. Part coming-of-age, part murder mystery, part love letter to the natural world and the cost of isolation."},
"Who We Are and How We Got Here": {k: "A geneticist reveals how ancient DNA analysis is rewriting human history — showing massive migrations, mixtures, and population replacements we never knew about. Reich demonstrates that modern populations are far more mixed than any pure-race narrative allows. A revolutionary but careful book about what our genes reveal about our shared, tangled ancestry."},
"Wounded Knee": {k: "Treuer reframes Native American history from 1890 to the present, challenging the narrative that indigenous cultures were destroyed at Wounded Knee. He documents resilience, adaptation, and cultural renaissance across reservations and urban communities. A powerful counter-narrative showing Native America as vibrant and ongoing, not a tragedy frozen in time."},
"You Can Thank Me Later": {k: "An unforgettable family navigated across three Thanksgivings, with all the dysfunction, love, and complicated dynamics that holiday gatherings bring out. Short, sweet, and emotionally satisfying. A cozy listen about family as both burden and gift."},
"Young Rich Widows": {k: "In 1985 Providence, four women lose their husbands when a mafia-connected law firm's private jet crashes, and quickly discover the men left behind more debts than assets. As they dig into their husbands' secrets, they uncover a dangerous conspiracy that puts their own lives at risk. A twisty, propulsive thriller about women underestimated by everyone around them."},
"Zero to One": {k: "Thiel argues that true innovation means creating something entirely new (going from 0 to 1), not copying what works (going from 1 to n). He champions monopoly over competition and contrarian thinking over consensus. A provocative startup philosophy book that challenges conventional wisdom about business and progress."}
};
